<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of Saint-Sernin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint-Sernin, Toulouse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of Saint-Sernin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> is a large Romanesque church built from about 1080 over the tomb of Saint Saturninus, the city's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> consecrated its altar in 1096. A major stop on the pilgrimage route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, it is one of the largest surviving Romanesque churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-sernin.jpg" alt="Basilica of Saint-Sernin" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilique_Saint-Sernin_de_Toulouse_-_exposition_ouest-1-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
